(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-micro-exam-1-timetable-weighted-quiz"]={"title":"Microbiology Exam 1 — Timetable-Weighted Quiz","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A Gram-positive organism is isolated from a wound. Which agent is it especially vulnerable to?","choices":["Nothing that acts on the wall","Polymyxin","An aminoglycoside alone","Lysozyme"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Its thick peptidoglycan is the enzyme's substrate and lies exposed as the outermost layer, with no outer membrane covering it. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 24-29"},{"q":"Which wall feature makes Gram-negative organisms resistant to many agents that kill Gram-positive ones?","choices":["An outer membrane","A thicker peptidoglycan layer","Teichoic acid","A capsule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It acts as a permeability barrier, excluding large and hydrophobic molecules before they can reach the peptidoglycan beneath. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 26-29"},{"q":"What is released from a Gram-negative organism when the cell lyses?","choices":["Exotoxin","Lipid A","Lysozyme","Teichoic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lipid A is the toxic portion of the lipopolysaccharide built into the outer membrane, so it only reaches the host when the cell breaks apart. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 26-29"},{"q":"What makes mycoplasmas unusual among bacteria?","choices":["They have two cell walls","They have no ribosomes","They have no cell wall","They have no genetic material"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The absence of peptidoglycan is the single feature from which everything else about them follows. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 14-18"},{"q":"Which structure allows a bacterium to survive heat, drying and disinfectants for years?","choices":["The plasmid","The capsule","The flagellum","The endospore"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is a dormant, dehydrated form with a tough protective coat that withstands conditions which kill the vegetative cell. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 30-36"},{"q":"Which structure allows a bacterium to resist being engulfed by a phagocyte?","choices":["The capsule","The endospore","Fimbriae","The ribosome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Its slippery polysaccharide layer prevents the phagocyte from gripping the surface, so the cell cannot be taken up. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 30-36"},{"q":"What does calling a medium 'selective' mean?","choices":["It suppresses the growth of unwanted organisms","It shows visible differences between organisms","It supplies extra nutrients for fastidious organisms","It maintains a constant incubation temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Something in the medium, such as a high salt concentration, prevents organisms other than the ones sought from growing at all. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 43-44"},{"q":"Which three results are read from blood agar?","choices":["Growth, no growth and partial growth","Acid, gas and no change","Coagulase, catalase and oxidase","Gamma, alpha and beta haemolysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each organism digests the haemoglobin to a different degree — none, partial, or complete — and the plate shows that difference directly. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 43-44"},{"q":"Bacterial growth refers to an increase in what?","choices":["Cell number","Cell size","Colony diameter","Genome size"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The individual cells stay much the same size; what rises is how many of them there are, through repeated binary fission. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 37-40"},{"q":"A disinfectant reduces a population from 10,000,000 organisms to 10,000. How many decimal reductions is that?","choices":["One thousand","Four","Six","Three"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each decimal reduction removes ninety per cent of whatever remains, so a thousandfold fall is three tenfold steps. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 62-68"},{"q":"A drug has a toxic dose for half the population of 400 milligrams and an effective dose for half the population of 50 milligrams. What is its therapeutic index?","choices":["350","0.125","8","450"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The index is the toxic dose divided by the effective dose, so 400 over 50 gives 8. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 18-20"},{"q":"What does a LARGER therapeutic index tell you about a drug?","choices":["It is more likely to be bactericidal","It is safer","It is more potent","It acts more quickly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A wide gap between the dose that works and the dose that harms leaves more room for error in dosing and for variation between patients. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 18-20"},{"q":"An antibiotic course is followed by an infection that no longer responds to it. What has happened to the bacterial population?","choices":["The immune system spared the resistant cells","The antibiotic caused resistance genes to form","The bacteria learned to avoid the drug","Resistant organisms already present were selected for"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The drug removes the susceptible competition rather than creating anything, so the few organisms that already carried resistance are left to multiply unopposed. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 45-52"},{"q":"Which resistance mechanism destroys the antibiotic molecule itself?","choices":["Target modification","An efflux pump","Enzymatic inactivation","Reduced permeability"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An enzyme such as beta-lactamase chemically cleaves the drug, so it can no longer bind its target however much of it is present. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 45-52"},{"q":"Which resistance mechanism keeps the antibiotic from ever reaching a useful concentration inside the cell?","choices":["Target modification","Enzymatic inactivation","Efflux pumping","Biofilm formation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pump exports the drug as fast as it enters, so it never accumulates to a level that would inhibit its target. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 45-52"},{"q":"Which bacterial target is completely absent from human cells?","choices":["The peptidoglycan cell wall","The ribosome","The plasma membrane","Deoxyribonucleic acid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nothing in a human cell corresponds to it, which is why drugs that attack it are among the safest antimicrobials available. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 24-32"},{"q":"Which step do the penicillins block?","choices":["Protein synthesis at the ribosome","Crosslinking of the cell wall","Folate synthesis","Deoxyribonucleic acid replication"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The beta-lactam ring inactivates the transpeptidase that links peptidoglycan strands, so the wall cannot be completed and the cell bursts. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 24-32"},{"q":"Why are there fewer drugs available against fungal and protozoal infections than against bacterial ones?","choices":["Those pathogens are eukaryotes, like us","Those pathogens reproduce too slowly","Those infections resolve on their own","Those pathogens have tougher cell walls"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Selective toxicity needs a difference to exploit, and a eukaryotic pathogen's structure and physiology closely resemble our own, so safe targets are scarce. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 12-16"},{"q":"What does a bacteriostatic drug do?","choices":["Kills the organism outright","Inhibits growth without killing","Blocks the organism from attaching to host cells","Prevents the organism from acquiring resistance genes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It halts replication and leaves the host's immune system to clear the organisms that are already there. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 33-36"},{"q":"By what mechanism does resident flora protect the host?","choices":["It raises local temperature to inhibit invaders","It secretes antibody against pathogens","It digests pathogens as they arrive","It occupies the niche first"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Competition for space and nutrients means a newly arrived pathogen finds nowhere to establish and nothing left to consume. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 8-16"},{"q":"Which body site carries the largest resident microbial population?","choices":["Small intestine","Oral cavity","Nasal passages","Large intestine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The colon holds by far the densest population in the body, which is why disturbing it with antibiotics has such marked consequences. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 8-16"},{"q":"Which of these is normally sterile?","choices":["Conjunctiva","Large intestine","Distal urethra","Cerebrospinal fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is an internal compartment that no organism should reach, which is why any growth from it is significant. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 8-16"},{"q":"What defines a TRUE pathogen?","choices":["It is always Gram-negative","It sickens a healthy person","It always produces an exotoxin","It is acquired only in hospital"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It carries enough virulence to overcome intact defences, which is precisely what an opportunist cannot do. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 18-24"},{"q":"When is endotoxin released?","choices":["When the organism is phagocytosed intact","Continuously, secreted by the living cell","Only during sporulation","When the bacterial cell lyses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is a structural component of the Gram-negative outer membrane, so it only reaches the host once the cell breaks apart. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"Which organisms produce endotoxin?","choices":["All bacteria equally","Gram-positive bacteria","Fungi","Gram-negative bacteria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Endotoxin is the lipid A portion of lipopolysaccharide, which is built into the Gram-negative outer membrane and found nowhere else. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"Which structure allows a bacterium to attach to host tissue?","choices":["The capsule","Fimbriae","The endospore","The ribosome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They are the adhesins that anchor the organism to a specific receptor on the host cell, without which it would simply be washed away. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 30-38"},{"q":"What distinguishes a sign from a symptom?","choices":["A sign is treatable; a symptom is not","A sign is felt by the patient; a symptom is observed","A sign appears early; a symptom appears late","A sign is observed; a symptom is felt by the patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The distinction is who perceives it, which is why a sign can be recorded objectively and a symptom must be reported. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 50-58"},{"q":"What is a sequela?","choices":["A second infection following the first","Lasting damage after the illness","The interval before symptoms appear","A relapse of the original illness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the permanent tissue or organ injury left behind once the acute illness has resolved, such as blindness following gonococcal conjunctivitis. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 50-58"},{"q":"Which step completes Koch's postulates once a laboratory isolate has produced disease in a healthy subject?","choices":["Re-isolate it from the new subject","Sequence the isolate's genome","Show it is absent in healthy people","Treat the subject and confirm recovery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The set closes by recovering the same organism from the newly infected subject, which is what ties the cultivated isolate to the disease it produced. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"An infectious agent arising from the patient's own resident flora is described as which of the following?","choices":["Endogenous","Exogenous","Zoonotic","Nosocomial"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The term means the agent originates from within the host, which is what happens when resident flora reach a site they do not normally occupy. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What does a small infectious dose fifty indicate about an organism?","choices":["Lower virulence","Greater virulence","A longer incubation period","A narrower host range"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fewer particles are needed to infect half of an exposed population, which is what a greater degree of pathogenicity means. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which sites are most commonly involved in hospital-acquired infection?","choices":["Mouth, oesophagus and stomach lining","Urinary tract, airway and surgical wounds","Bone, joint spaces and middle ear","Liver, spleen and pancreatic duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each of the three is routinely instrumented, so catheters, ventilation and surgery breach the barrier that would otherwise exclude organisms. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What distinguishes a sign from a symptom?","choices":["A sign is reported by the patient","A sign appears only in severe disease","A sign is observed objectively","A sign appears before the incubation period"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is objective evidence noted by an observer and can often be measured, which is what separates it from what the patient reports. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How many links make up the chain of infection?","choices":["Eight","Four","Six","Three"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry and susceptible host, and breaking any one of them interrupts spread. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 6-14"},{"q":"Which link in the chain of infection does hand hygiene break?","choices":["The susceptible host","The reservoir","The mode of transmission","The portal of exit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Washing removes organisms from the hands that would otherwise carry them from one host to the next, which is the link most readily interrupted. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 6-14"},{"q":"What is a fomite?","choices":["A contaminated non-living object","A living carrier showing no symptoms","An airborne droplet nucleus","An insect that transmits disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is any inanimate item that carries organisms from one person to another, such as a stethoscope or a keyboard. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 40-52"},{"q":"What distinguishes a PASSIVE carrier from an active one?","choices":["The passive carrier has been treated","The passive carrier shows no symptoms","The passive carrier is not infected","The passive carrier transmits only by air"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They simply transfer organisms on hands or clothing without the organism ever establishing in them, which is why hand hygiene matters so much among staff. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 16-26"},{"q":"What separates a vector from a reservoir?","choices":["The vector carries the organism; the reservoir harbours it","The vector harbours the organism; the reservoir carries it","The vector is inanimate; the reservoir is living","The vector is always an insect; the reservoir always an animal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The reservoir is where the organism normally lives and multiplies, while the vector merely transports it to a new host. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 16-26"},{"q":"Why can a zoonotic infection not be eradicated the way smallpox was?","choices":["Zoonotic organisms resist all antimicrobials","Zoonotic organisms mutate too quickly","Zoonotic infections are always viral","The animal reservoir persists"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Eradication requires removing every place the organism can live, and an infection maintained in animals continues however many human cases are cured. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 28-36"},{"q":"Which patients are most at risk from hospital-acquired infection?","choices":["Outpatients attending clinics","The young and otherwise healthy","The immunocompromised","Patients on short admissions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Impaired defences allow organisms of low virulence, including the patient's own flora, to establish infection they could not otherwise cause. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"Contaminated drinking water spreads infection through which route?","choices":["Direct contact","Vehicle","Vector","Fomite"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Water is one of the named vehicles, alongside food, soil and air, each carrying the agent to a host with no contact between people. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Person-to-person spread from a mother to her child is termed which of the following?","choices":["Horizontal transmission","Indirect contact","Vertical transmission","Vehicle transmission"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vertical transmission is the mother-to-child route, passing down a generation rather than between members of the same one. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which of the three lines of defense is the specific one?","choices":["All three equally","The first","The second","The third"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Only the third is acquired through exposure to a particular antigen and carries memory of it; the first two act identically against anything. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Into which first-line category does lysozyme fall?","choices":["Microbiological","Physical","Chemical","Genetic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is an enzyme that digests peptidoglycan, attacking the invader chemically rather than obstructing it. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 13-18"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is the most numerous?","choices":["The monocyte","The lymphocyte","The neutrophil","The eosinophil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils make up roughly 55 to 90 per cent of the differential, which is why a raised total count so often reflects a neutrophil response. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 66-70"},{"q":"What can a macrophage do that a neutrophil cannot?","choices":["Release histamine","Phagocytose bacteria","Migrate towards a chemical signal","Present antigen to lymphocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is the step that recruits the specific immune system, which is why the macrophage bridges innate and adaptive defence while the neutrophil simply kills and dies. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 66-72"},{"q":"What does the differential add that a total white cell count alone cannot give?","choices":["Whether the cell types are in normal proportion","The total number of white cells, more precisely","The identity of the infecting organism","The antibody titre against the organism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A normal total can conceal a badly skewed mix, and it is the proportion that distinguishes a bacterial from a viral pattern. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 70-74"},{"q":"Which set lists the four classical signs of inflammation?","choices":["Swelling, pain, itching and scaling","Redness, heat, swelling and pain","Heat, swelling, bleeding and numbness","Redness, heat, pus and fever"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rubor, calor, tumor and dolor, with loss of function sometimes added as a fifth. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 34-40"},{"q":"What causes fever?","choices":["Endotoxin acting directly on the skin","Dehydration raising core temperature","Histamine released from mast cells","Cytokines reset the set point"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Interleukin-1, interleukin-6 and tumour necrosis factor act systemically on the hypothalamus, which then defends a higher target temperature. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"What does interferon do to a virus?","choices":["It destroys viral particles directly","It prevents spread rather than killing the virus","It coats the virus so phagocytes engulf it","It lyses the infected cell immediately"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It acts on neighbouring uninfected cells, inducing an antiviral state so the infection cannot propagate; the already-infected cell is not rescued. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 48-52"},{"q":"In what order are the three complement pathways activated?","choices":["Classical, then lectin, then alternative","Alternative, then lectin, then classical","Lectin, then alternative, then classical","Classical, then alternative, then lectin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The alternative pathway fires immediately with no prior recognition needed, the lectin pathway follows, and the classical comes last because it waits on antibody. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 42-46"},{"q":"A newborn receives antibody across the placenta. Which type of immunity is that?","choices":["Artificial passive","Natural active","Natural passive","Artificial active"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nothing was engineered, so it is natural, and the infant receives ready-made antibody rather than producing it, so it is passive and temporary. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 6-12"},{"q":"Which type of immunity does vaccination produce?","choices":["Artificial active","Artificial passive","Natural active","Natural passive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The antigen is administered deliberately, so it is artificial, and the recipient mounts their own response and forms memory, so it is active. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 6-12"},{"q":"How many chains make up an antibody monomer?","choices":["Six","Eight","Four","Two"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two identical heavy chains and two identical light chains form the Y-shaped molecule. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 40-48"},{"q":"Which antibody class appears first in a new infection?","choices":["Immunoglobulin A","Immunoglobulin E","Immunoglobulin M","Immunoglobulin G"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is produced first and is by far the largest, its ten binding sites making it highly effective at agglutinating antigen early on. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 50-60"},{"q":"Which antibody class guards mucosal surfaces?","choices":["Immunoglobulin M","Immunoglobulin A","Immunoglobulin D","Immunoglobulin E"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is secreted across epithelium onto the mucous membranes of the gut, airway and urogenital tract, where it blocks attachment. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 50-60"},{"q":"When are lymphocyte receptor specificities generated?","choices":["After the antigen arrives, in response to it","Before any antigen has entered","Only during an active infection","Continuously, in proportion to antigen exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The repertoire is produced during lymphocyte development by gene rearrangement, so an arriving antigen selects a clone that already exists rather than instructing anything. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 30-38"},{"q":"Class two major histocompatibility complex molecules present antigen to which cell?","choices":["The helper T cell","The cytotoxic T cell","The B cell","The natural killer cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class two is carried by antigen-presenting cells and displays engulfed material to the helper subset, which bears the matching cluster of differentiation 4 marker. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 20-28"},{"q":"What does opsonisation do?","choices":["Coats the organism so phagocytes can engulf it","Blocks the organism's attachment sites","Clumps organisms together","Punches holes in the organism's membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The coating marks the target and gives the phagocyte a surface it can bind, which greatly increases the rate of engulfment. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 62-70"},{"q":"How does a second exposure to the same antigen differ from the first?","choices":["It is identical to the first","It is slower and produces more immunoglobulin M","It is faster and produces far more immunoglobulin G","It is weaker, because tolerance has developed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Memory cells laid down by the first exposure are already present and pre-committed, so the response begins sooner, climbs higher and switches class. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 72-80"},{"q":"Which molecule must an antigen-presenting cell use to activate a helper T cell?","choices":["Class one major histocompatibility complex","Class two major histocompatibility complex","Class three major histocompatibility complex","Surface immunoglobulin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the immune regulatory receptors carried by antigen-presenting cells, and they are required to bring bound antigen to a helper T cell. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which T lymphocyte destroys virally infected cells by secreting enzymes that lyse them?","choices":["The cytotoxic T cell","The helper T cell","The suppressor T cell","The delayed hypersensitivity cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It lyses its targets directly and especially seeks out virally infected cells, cancer cells and cells from another individual. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which antibody reaches a far higher level on a second exposure to the same antigen?","choices":["Immunoglobulin M","Immunoglobulin A","Immunoglobulin E","Immunoglobulin G"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Memory cells drive the secondary response and produce a much higher titre of this class, with immunoglobulin M rising slowly behind it. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A vaccine made from a purified, inactivated bacterial toxin is described as which of the following?","choices":["A toxoid","A subunit preparation","A live attenuated preparation","A killed whole-cell preparation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A toxoid is built from purified antigenic components of a toxin, keeping the antigenicity while the toxic activity is destroyed. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Why does protection from donated antibodies last only a few months?","choices":["The antibodies are destroyed by complement","No memory cells are produced","The dose is too small to protect","The recipient makes antibodies against them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The recipient is given finished antibody without ever mounting a response, so nothing remains to reproduce the protection once it decays. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
